    "q-65128": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65128",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'.",
