    "q-65129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65129",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$",
      "worked_solution": "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
