    "q-80759": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80759",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
