    "q-133393": {
      "dom_id": "step-q133393",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zz}(x,y,z)=2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}) \\\\  & =2z\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}+{e}^{1-2\\cdot 1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-1}+{e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
